text:
emma: ran an nsys + perfetto trace for a few long requests and something odd popped up. KV cache prefetch kernels are showing up outside the request NVTX ranges, so timeline stitching and per-request latency attribution are wrong :thinking_face:

raj: can you paste the perfetto link / snapshot? curious if it's a sampling artifact or our instrumentation ordering.

emma: https://perfetto.dev/trace?example=2451234567 (uploaded the trace, small sample)

emma: quick summary — dispatch -> select_kernel -> launch_kernel sequence. We currently push a single nvtx range at the top-level request boundary, but kernel selection and the cudaLaunch appear to be instrumented in different threads/processes which makes the kernel's NVTX not nested. Result: KV prefetch kernels attributed to the generic dispatcher range instead of the actual request/kernel variant.

oliver: yep seen that before. causality breaks when we call into the device driver threads. NVTX ranges are per-thread; if the work hops threads (or the profiler groups kernels by launch correlation) they won't show up nested.

maria: for a minimal fix we can add two ranges: one around kernel-selection (synchronous CPU work) and one immediately before cudaLaunch (so the launch correlates to the right parent). naming convention should be deterministic: e.g. "req:<route>:<reqid>" and "kern_sel:<op>:<variant>". keeps parsing simple for perf scripts. :white_check_mark:

emma: proposed naming example (psuedo code):

```cpp
// at request entry
char buf[128];
snprintf(buf, sizeof(buf), "req:%s:%lu", route, req_id);
nvtxRangePushA(buf);

// before kernel selection
char ks[128];
snprintf(ks, sizeof(ks), "kern_sel:%s:%s", op_name, selector_key);
nvtxRangePushA(ks);
// do selection...
nvtxRangePop(); // kern_sel

// just before kernel launch (on the thread that does launch)
char kl[128];
snprintf(kl, sizeof(kl), "kern_launch:%s:%s", op_name, kernel_variant);
nvtxRangePushA(kl);
// launch kernel
nvtxRangePop(); // kern_launch

// at request exit
nvtxRangePop(); // req
```

raj: that looks good. we should also include a stable short id for the kernel variant (not the full path) so scripts can group them. avoid ephemeral pointers.

oliver: slight nuance — we sometimes batch multiple logical requests into a single kernel launch (continuous batching). for those cases kern_launch should include a batch descriptor: "kern_launch:<variant>:batch=<n>" so perf tools and dashboards can compute per-request amortized cost.

maria: agreed. also add a small tag when KV cache prefetch happens: "kv_prefetch:true" in the range name or as an extra nvtxRangePush with name "kv_prefetch" so it's easy to filter.

emma: perf-bot uploaded nsys capture: /artifacts/perf/nsys/2451234567.nsys (linked in trace). ran nvtx-quality check script and it flagged 12 kernels with missing parent ranges.

perf-bot: summary: 12 orphan kernels detected across 3 request traces. suggested rules: ensure kernel launch occurs while request range is active OR add explicit kernel launch range as above.

oliver: action items:
 - (oliver) add kern_sel and kern_launch NVTX pushes in runtime where selection/launch happens (PR + unit test)
 - (emma) add perf regression test that runs sample trace and asserts no orphan kernels
 - (raj) update perfetto/trace parsing script to support new naming pattern and batch annotation

maria: small naming guideline proposal to add to docs: use colon-separated stable tokens, avoid ioctl/pointer strings, and append lightweight annotations as key=value segments. examples: "req:chat:1234", "kern_launch:matmul:v2:batch=4:kv_prefetch=true"

emma: putting a short RFC in doc PR now. I'll reference the trace and attach minimal diff. cc @oliver @raj for review. :eyes:

raj: one more thought — if kernel selection runs in a different executor thread pool, we should consider piggybacking a small correlation id via the kernel launch stream (if runtime supports it) so downstream profilers like Nsight can correlate asynchronous activity. but that's a follow-up.

oliver: agreed follow-up. for now we'll implement NVTX ranges as discussed and iterate. thanks team.

maria: noted and added jira PERF-8471 to track follow-up correlation id work.

emma: thanks all, rolling PR later today.
